message: "If you use this software or the study's measurements, please cite it as below."
title: "Authored-Plan Agents: Reference Implementation and Measurement Study of an Architectural Pattern for LLM Agent Platforms"
type: software
license: MIT
authors:
  - family-names: "Senhor Ferreira"
    given-names: "Estefano"
    email: "estefano.senhor@gmail.com"
date-released: "2026-08-06"
version: "0.3.0"
keywords:
  - LLM agents
  - agent orchestration
  - architectural patterns
  - telemetry blindness
  - gray failure
  - saga compensation
  - structured output
  - false success
abstract: >
  Reference implementation of an architectural pattern for LLM agent platforms
  in which non-determinism is confined to selection and generation, never to
  sequence, with a restriction-enforcing test suite and four rounds of
  real-model measurement over a persisted external system of record. Central
  measured finding: telemetry blindness -- every platform-side observer
  reports success while the business database holds corrupted state --
  demonstrated in three model families and under two decoding conditions,
  including a pre-registered follow-up whose opposite prediction the data
  falsified (fence-stripping repair absorbs rather than extracts). Decoding-
  level structured output suppressed every natural violation measured
  (bounded observations, not elimination claims); the architectural output-
  contract guard is what keeps the channel closed when that mechanism
  silently breaks, at zero measured idle cost. Selection at catalog scale:
  ambiguity behaves as a floor across the measured range rather than a
  catalog-size slope (small cells; qualitative reading), out-of-catalog
  refusal showed zero errors on its probes with stated bounds, and the
  id-only selection contract's robustness proved to be a model-family
  property, not a task property. Every absence claim is search- and
  time-bound; falsified predictions are part of the record.
